sascha Alexeyev ax never goes anywhere  without her bag she makes a new  political poster for it every week and  takes it with her on her commute  whether it's sexism freedom of speech or  this call to donate bone marrow members  of teehee pickets the silent protest  movement take on any topic they think  needs public discussion in Russia sasha  was one of the first of around 80 young  women who started taking political  posters with them everywhere they went  and then sharing their experiences  online our goal is to create a  politically aware civil society the  problem in Russia is that everyone is  hoping for some kind of Messiah someone  who will lead us all to a revolution but  that won't happen you need a lot of  people normal people not heroes to see  themselves as political actors and  change something in today's Russia even  carrying posters on social issues as a  political act and voicing dissent is  getting harder according to recent laws  even peaceful protestors like Marvel  p.m. can be imprisoned for up to 5 years  Palpatine has been actively involved in  anti-government protests since early  2011 there's absolutely no freedom of  assembly if you want to organize a  political gathering or a protest then in  the majority of cases the authorities  won't give you permission as Russian  parliamentary elections approached Sasha  and Darya City Anka the founder of  ticket hope their posters will get  people thinking about politics as well  we don't have the resources the  government has we don't have access to  television  we don't have funding we're just trying  to do what we can and that means  changing things won't be a quick process  to process  Sasha wants her words to make people  think she hopes to change minds one  poster at a time
 

